# Security Standards

## OpenRouter API key

`OPENROUTER_API_KEY` is a server-only secret. It must never appear in client-side code.

- No `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix -- that would expose it to the browser
- Only read in `app/api/` route handlers or `lib/ai/openrouter.ts` (which is only ever called server-side)
- Never import `lib/ai/openrouter.ts` from a component or hook

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — readable by anyone who opens devtools
const key = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_OPENROUTER_API_KEY

// ✅ GOOD — server-only, undefined in the browser
const key = process.env.OPENROUTER_API_KEY
```

## All AI calls go through the API route

Components and hooks never call OpenRouter directly. The flow is always:

```
component → hook → fetch("/api/generate") → app/api/generate/route.ts → OpenRouter
```

If you find yourself importing `openrouter.ts` into a hook or component, stop and route it through an API handler instead.

## Environment variables

- Never hardcode secrets, keys, or tokens in source code
- Keep a `.env.local` file locally (already in `.gitignore`)
- Document required variables in `.env.example` with placeholder values -- commit that file

```bash
# .env.example
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=your_key_here
```

## User input

Any text sent to the AI (the vibe prompt) passes through the API route. Trim and limit its length server-side before forwarding to OpenRouter -- do not trust the client to enforce this.
